.TH BADBLK 1M UniSoft
.SH NAME
badblk \- program to set or update bad block information
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B badblk
[
.B \-w
] [
.B \-m
N] /dev/rXYZ [ 
.B \#S
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Badblk
sets or updates bad block information for those disk drives that
support soft sector bad block remapping.
.PP
If invoked with the
.B \-w
option, write/verify is performed to determine if there is a bad block;
otherwise only read is done.
.PP
If invoked with the
.BR \-m N
option, the number of alternate blocks will be set to N.
.I Badblk
returns an error if N > NICALT (currently 50).
.RS .3i
.TP "\w'/dev/rXYZ\ \ \ 'u"
.B /dev/rXYZ
is the device name.
.TP
.B #S
is one or more block numbers separated by blanks.
.RE
.PP
If invoked with no specific block numbers and no bad block verification
has been done before, then each block on the disk is checked
(either read or write/verify) and bad block information
in block 0 is set up from scratch.
.PP
If invoked with no specific block numbers, but block 0 already contains
bad block information set up earlier, then a verification on the whole
disk is performed; any new bad blocks not already on the block 0
table will be added.
.PP
If invoked with the device name plus block numbers, then only the
indicated blocks are updated in block 0.
.PP
After alternate blocks are assigned, block 0 is updated and the 
updated blocks are verified to make sure alternate blocks are good.
If alternate blocks are not good, new alternate block numbers
are assigned.
.PP
The raw device that accesses the entire disk and allows for writing
block zero should be specified.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
badblk -w /dev/rw1hw0
.PP
does a full write/verify on winchester 1 and updates the header block.
The rw1hw0 specifies raw (r) winchester 1 (w1), the full disk (h),
with the capability of writing block 0 (w0).
.IP
badblk /dev/rw1hw0 3754 8123
.PP
adds blocks 3754 and 8123 to the badblock list.
.\"	@(#)badblk.1m	5.1 of 10/18/83
